\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Trace generation.} The LLM generates $K$ traces $\{t_1, \ldots, t_K\}$ at temperature $T{=}0.7$, each producing a candidate answer $a_i$.

\paragraph{Constraint extraction.} The same LLM extracts structured FOL constraints $C_i$ from each trace $t_i$ at $T_{\text{ext}}{=}0.3$. While we prompt for FOL-style formalizations, extracted constraints are predominantly ground propositional clauses without quantifiers. The extraction prompt requests JSON output containing a human-readable expression and a Z3-compatible formula string. Each constraint $c$ is associated with its source trace and answer. The \emph{extraction rate} is the fraction of traces yielding at least one parseable constraint; traces with no valid constraints are excluded from MAX-SAT but still contribute answers.

\paragraph{Deduplication.} Constraints are deduplicated by syntactic equivalence. Each unique constraint $c_m$ receives weight $w_m$ equal to the number of distinct traces that produced it.

\paragraph{Weighted partial MAX-SAT.} Let $\mathcal{A}(q)$ denote the set of distinct candidate answers and $\mathcal{C} = \{c_1, \ldots, c_M\}$ the deduplicated constraint pool. Each constraint serves as a soft clause; the solver finds the largest-weight satisfiable subset:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:maxsat}
\mathcal{C}^* = \arg\max_{\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{C}} \sum_{c \in \mathcal{S}} w_c \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathcal{S} \text{ is satisfiable.}
\end{equation}
Z3 \citep{demoura2008z3} solves this with a 10-second per-problem timeout. Each candidate answer is scored by:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:score}
\text{score}(a) = \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^*} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a) - \alpha \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^{\setminus}} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(c, a) = 1$ if $c$ was extracted from any trace producing answer $a$, $\mathcal{C}^{\setminus} = \mathcal{C} \setminus \mathcal{C}^*$, and $\alpha = 0.5$. An answer assertion $\texttt{answer\_a} == \texttt{True}$ with weight 1 is injected for each candidate, making SICA a strict generalization of SC.

\paragraph{Degeneration to vote counting.} Self-extracted constraints are tightly coupled to their source answer ($\sigma(c_m, a_i) \approx 1$). When constraints lack discriminative power across candidates, MAX-SAT excludes almost nothing ($|\mathcal{C}^{\setminus}| \approx 0$), and the score degenerates to a weighted trace count equivalent to majority voting (confirmed empirically in \S\ref{sec:ablation}). We define the multi-candidate rate $C_0$ as the fraction of questions with more than one distinct answer across $K$ traces; when $C_0 < 5\%$, SICA and SC produce identical output by construction.

\subsection{Oracle Control Design}
\label{sec:oracle-design}

To disentangle constraint quality from the aggregation mechanism, we construct an oracle variant that replaces self-extracted constraints with gold annotations: expert-authored FOL formulas for FOLIO \citep{han2022folio} and programmatic DSL rules for ProofWriter \citep{tafjord2021proofwriter}. Z3 evaluates the gold constraints against each candidate answer from the same $K$ traces used by SC and self-extraction SICA. This creates a causal intervention: if oracle-SICA improves over SC but self-extraction SICA does not, the bottleneck lies in extraction quality rather than in the aggregation mechanism.

\subsection{Scope}
\label{sec:scope}

SICA as evaluated here uses \emph{same-model self-extraction}: the same LLM generates traces and extracts constraints. Methods using trained extractors, external provers, or multi-model pipelines are out of scope; our goal is to test whether a single LLM's reasoning can be usefully self-formalized for aggregation. We evaluate nine remediation strategies spanning prompting, architecture, capability, verification, diversity, and filtering dimensions (\S\ref{sec:remediation}).

